EXT. SUNSET GARDENS RETIREMENT HOME - BOCCE COURT - DAY

Crushed oyster-shell lane, green benches, a hand-painted banner: LEAGUE FINALS. Folding chairs full of cardigans.

ALBERTINA "BERTIE" MOSCATO, 80, pearls, steel spine, rolls her wrist like a bishop blessing. LOU "THE HAMMER" GENOVESE, 82, undershirt, arms like cured hams, glares from the other end.

Bertie lets fly. The ball skims, kisses the pallino. Applause.

BERTIE
Beat that, Hammer!

LOU
I've beaten better, Bertie!

HIRO NAKAMURA, 78, referee, pressed shirt, kneels with a tape measure. He measures. Looks up.

HIRO
Bertie leads by half an inch.

DORIS PELLEGRINO, 75, Lou's partner, sun visor, mutters.

DORIS
Half an inch. I've had longer lunches.

Lou steps to the line. Spits on his palm. Rolls. The ball veers left, strikes Bertie's ball, scatters it. The crowd gasps.

LOU
Ha!

BERTIE
Cheat!

HIRO
Legal. Lou leads.

ORVILLE "SKIP" TATE, 85, Bertie's partner, wheelchair, a blanket across his knees, rolls up.

SKIP
Bertie, you owe me three dollars if you lose.

BERTIE
Skip, I never lose.

SKIP
Ninety-one losses, Bertie. I counted.

BERTIE
Quiet, you old goat.

She picks up the last ball. Stares down the court. Quiet descends. A wind chime tinkles.

She steps. Swings. Lets go.

The ball curves, ticks the fence, rolls, and stops dead against the pallino.

Silence. Then an uproar.

DORIS
Hiro, it's wood. Look at the grain!

HIRO
Bertie wins.

LOU
(low)
Rematch.

BERTIE
Next Sunday, Hammer. Bring your checkbook.
